var e={journey:{canvasLabel:`The signal path: a sound reaches a microphone (the KY-037's voltage into the ADC, or the INMP441's numbers over I2S), the ESP32-S3 works on it, and the lab turns it into something else, often a sound on the speaker.`,optional:`{{part}} (optional)`,output:`→ {{out}}`,path:{noMic:`(no mic)`,adcOrI2s:`ADC or I2S0`,withSpeaker:`16 000/s · I2S1 out`},parts:{ky:`KY-037`,inmp:`INMP441`,mic:`KY-037 or INMP441`,spk:`MAX98357A + speaker`,wifi:`Wi-Fi (W1)`},labs:{S1:{title:`Hear`,what:`the raw signal: scope, both mics side by side, bias or bits, the comparator`,out:`screen`},S2:{title:`Loudness`,what:`a level meter (dB SPL with the INMP441), a 10-minute diary, an alarm`,out:`LED · tune`},S3:{title:`Clap control`,what:`claps (not voices) in patterns of 1-4 switch things`,out:`LED · RGB · sounds`},S4:{title:`Recorder`,what:`voice-activated recording; listen on your phone or the board`,out:`Wi-Fi · WAV · speaker`},S5:{title:`Spectrum`,what:`our own FFT: bands, a waterfall, a tuner with a reference tone`,out:`pitch`},S6:{title:`Sound games`,what:`a knock lock, Clap Bird, a beat-following party`,out:`games · sounds`},S7:{title:`Speaker`,what:`a synth: waves, notes, chords, envelopes, tunes, game sounds`,out:`the speaker`},S8:{title:`Voice FX`,what:`a parrot that repeats you as a chipmunk or a robot; live echo`,out:`the speaker`}}},controls:{play:`▶ Play`,pause:`❚❚ Pause`,speed:`speed ×{{speed}}`,restart:`↺ restart`},sounds:{talk:`🗣 talk`,whistle:`〰 whistle`,clap:`👏 clap`,quiet:`🤫 quiet`},chain:{label:`A pipeline of eight stages from air pressure to decibels, with the selected stage drawn live below.`,previous:`Previous stage`,next:`Next stage`,position:`{{at}} / {{total}}`,steps:{air:{box:`air`,title:`1 · A sound is moving air`,text:`Your voice pushes air molecules together and pulls them apart, hundreds of times a second. The pressure swings by about 0.02 Pa when you talk 30 cm away, on top of the 101 325 Pa the air always has. That tiny swing is all the microphone gets.`},capsule:{box:`capsule`,title:`2 · The capsule turns it into volts`,text:`The KY-037's silver can is an electret capsule: a thin charged foil (the diaphragm) in front of a metal plate. The air moves the foil, the gap changes, and the charge makes a voltage of a few millivolts. There is no amplifier on this module: what you get is that small.`},ao:{box:`AO pin`,title:`3 · On the AO pin: a small wave on a big steady voltage`,text:`The capsule needs power, so its output sits on a steady DC voltage, the <b>bias</b> (around 1.5 V here; S1's bias view shows yours). The sound is a ripple of a few mV on top. At true scale you can't see it: the inset magnifies it 100 times.`},adc:{box:`ADC`,title:`4 · The ADC measures it 16 000 times a second`,text:`The ADC turns the voltage into a number from 0 to 4095. With 12 dB attenuation the range is 0-3.1 V, so one step is about 0.76 mV and a whisper is only a few steps tall. Smaller attenuation = a smaller range = finer steps (S1's <code>atten</code>).`},dma:{box:`DMA`,title:`5 · The DMA collects results with no CPU`,text:`In continuous mode the ADC's own timer starts each conversion, and the DMA (direct memory access) writes every result, 4 bytes, into RAM by itself. After 256 results (1 KB, 16 ms) it hands the frame to our sampler task on core 0. The screen loop on core 1 never waits for it.`},ring:{box:`ring`,title:`6 · A 4-second ring of samples`,text:`The sampler writes every sample into a ring of 65 536 slots in PSRAM. Each sample has a running number; the slot is that number modulo 65 536, so the ring overwrites itself every 4.1 s. The scope, the FFT and the recorder read stretches out of it by number, whenever they like.`},bias:{box:`− bias`,title:`7 · Remove the bias`,text:`A very slow average follows the bias (it takes about a quarter of a second to settle). Each sample minus that average is the sound alone: a wave around zero. The pot, a different module or a warm room can move the bias; the average simply follows.`},db:{box:`int16 → dB`,title:`8 · Numbers a sound program understands`,text:`×16 turns ADC steps into 16-bit audio units (−32 768 to 32 767, like a WAV file). Every 64 samples (4 ms) the sampler measures the frame's RMS, the average strength, and the level meter turns it into decibels: compared with the ADC's full scale (dBFS) and with your room's quiet.`}},air:{value:`crowded = pressure up, sparse = down · {{db, number(minimumFractionDigits: 0; maximumFractionDigits: 0)}} dB above the quiet right now`},capsule:{diaphragm:`diaphragm`,plate:`plate`,box:`capsule output, last 40 ms (±30 mV box)`,value:`capsule now: {{mv, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2; signDisplay: exceptZero)}} mV`},ao:{bias:`bias {{v, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} V: the wave is in this line, too small to see`,zoom:`×100 around the bias (±30 mV)`,valuePlus:`AO now: {{v, number(minimumFractionDigits: 4; maximumFractionDigits: 4)}} V = {{bias}} mV bias + {{mv, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} mV of sound`,valueMinus:`AO now: {{v, number(minimumFractionDigits: 4; maximumFractionDigits: 4)}} V = {{bias}} mV bias − {{mv, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} mV of sound`},adc:{dots:`40 samples = 2.5 ms · one dot every 62.5 µs · each line = one ADC step = {{mv, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} mV`,value:`raw now: {{raw}} of 0-4095 · attenuation {{atten}}`},dma:{frame:`DMA frame: 256 results × 4 bytes`,sampler:`sampler`,samplerCore:`task, core 0`,loopCore:`screen, core 1`,idle:`the CPU does nothing while a frame fills`,value:`62.5 frames a second, 16 ms each · the sampler wakes only when one is full`},ring:{write:`write`,readers:{scope:`● scope: reads the newest 20 ms`,fft:`● FFT: reads the newest 512 samples`,recorder:`● recorder: reads half a second back (its pre-roll)`},size:`65 536 slots · 128 KB of PSRAM · one lap = 4.1 s`,value:`written = {{w, number}} · slot = written & 65535 = {{slot}}`},bias:{raw:`raw (ADC steps) and its slow average: {{dc, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}}`,minus:`− average  ↓`,alone:`the sound alone, around 0`,value:`raw {{raw}} − average {{dc, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} = {{diff, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} steps`},db:{frames:`8 frames of 64 samples (4 ms each) · ×16 = int16 units`,value:`fast {{fast, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} dBFS · floor {{floor, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} dBFS · {{above, number(minimumFractionDigits: 0; maximumFractionDigits: 0)}} dB above the quiet`}},mics:{label:`Top: the KY-037's analog path and the INMP441's digital path. Below: an I2S frame on the SCK, WS and SD wires, a cursor reading the sample bit by bit, and the sample turned into a 16-bit number.`,tickLabel:`One clock tick`,tick:`tick ▶`,pressure:`sound pressure`,pressureLabel:`The sample's value, as a share of full scale`,output:`{{s24}} (24-bit)`,caption:{reading:`Each tick of SCK, the mic puts one bit on SD; the ESP32 reads it on the rising edge. WS says whose turn it is: low = the left slot.`,shift:`Why >> 6 and not >> 8 more? Taking the top 16 bits would leave a quiet room near zero. Keeping 6 more low bits is 12 dB of gain: a voice uses real numbers, and only a clap right at the mic (about 114 dB SPL) clips.`},lane:{capsule:`capsule`,fewMv:`a few mV`,plusBias:`+ bias`,onAo:`~1.5 V on AO`,adcBits:`12-bit, 0.76 mV`,minusAverage:`− average`,dmaRing:`DMA, ring`,ownAmp:`+ its own amp`,adc24:`24-bit ADC`,insideMic:`inside the mic`,frames:`I2S frames`,sample24:`24-bit sample`},frame:{left:`left slot (L/R → GND: the mic talks here)`,right:`right slot: nobody (zeros)`,firstBit:`bit 23 (the sign) first … bit 0`,padding:`8 padding zeros`},read:{soFar:`read so far: {{got}} of 24 bits`,level:`that's {{dbfs, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} dBFS; ≈ {{spl, number(minimumFractionDigits: 0; maximumFractionDigits: 0)}} dB SPL for a sine (dBFS + 111, from the datasheet's -26 dBFS at 94 dB SPL)`,clock:`SCK = 16 000 samples × 2 slots × 32 bits = 1.024 MHz, driven by the ESP32 (I2S0); the speaker uses I2S1`}},bias:{label:`A 0 to 3.3 volt ruler with the ADC's range shaded, the bias line and the microphone's wave; parts outside the range are clipped.`,bias:`bias`,biasLabel:`Bias voltage in millivolts`,sound:`sound`,soundLabel:`How loud: wave height`,atten:`Attenuation`,attenOption:`atten {{db}} dB`,caption:`Bias {{bias}} mV, sound ±{{a}} mV, attenuation {{atten}}. The hint is the same one S1's bias view prints on the board.`,range:`ADC range 0-{{v, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} V`,steps:`1 ADC step = {{mv, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} mV · this wave spans {{steps}} of 4096 steps`,hint:{clipping:`CLIPPING: the wave hits the range's edge. Flat tops = lost sound (orange); red = what was cut off.`,nearTop:`bias near the top: use a bigger atten`,try6:`fits in 0-1.75 V: try atten 6 (2x finer)`,try25:`fits in 0-1.25 V: try atten 2.5`,good:`the wave fits the range: good`}},comparator:{label:`The AO wave over the last two seconds with the pot's threshold line, and the DO output below it: high wherever the wave is above the line.`,potRange:`pot: threshold above the bias`,potLabel:`Comparator threshold in millivolts above the bias`,drag:`drag the dashed line too`,caption:`Threshold {{pot}} mV above the bias. Each wave crest above the dashed line is one rising edge on DO (amber); a clap makes dozens, which is why S3 listens to AO, not DO.`,axis:`AO minus the bias, last 4 s (square-root scale)`,pot:`pot {{mv}} mV`,perSecond:`{{n}}/s`,hint:{alwaysOn:`DO always on: turn the pot the other way`,underLine:`even the clap stays under the line: turn the pot back`,waiting:`nothing loud yet: a clap is coming`,tuned:`DO follows the loud moments: tuned`}},ballistics:{label:`A replay of a sound scene: the instant level, the fast and slow meters, the peak hold and the noise floor over time.`,scene:`Scene`,scenes:{talk:`talk, then a clap`,fan:`a fan switches on for 30 s`},legend:{inst:`instant (4 ms)`,fast:`fast 125 ms`,slow:`slow 1 s`,floor:`floor`,peak:`peak hold`},now:`{{s, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} s · {{db, number(minimumFractionDigits: 0; maximumFractionDigits: 0)}} dB above the quiet`,caption:{fan:`The fan adds a steady hum. The floor (teal) climbs 0.5 dB a second until the hum becomes the new quiet, so S2 stops calling it loud. When the fan stops, the floor drops at once. The clap at the end still stands out.`,talk:`Fast (pink) follows the syllables; slow (amber) shows the average loudness of the talk. The clap's instant level is far above both: a 4 ms bang barely moves a 1 s average. The peak hold (white ticks) keeps it 1.5 s, then falls 20 dB a second.`}},decibels:{label:`Two waves: the room's quiet and a louder sound you choose, with a decibel ruler showing that +6 dB doubles the height and +20 dB is ten times.`,howLoud:`how loud`,howLoudLabel:`Decibels above the quiet, times ten`,tooTall:`(too tall to draw: really {{px}} px)`,legend:`grey = the quiet of your room · pink = this sound`,marks:{quiet:`quiet`,rustle:`rustle`,talking:`talking`,clap:`clap`},caption:`{{db, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} dB above the quiet: the wave is {{ratio, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} times taller (power ×{{power, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}}). S2 calls this "{{like}}". Each +6 dB doubles the height; +20 dB is ten times.`,captionBig:`{{db, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} dB above the quiet: the wave is {{ratio, number(minimumFractionDigits: 0; maximumFractionDigits: 0)}} times taller (power ×{{power, number(minimumFractionDigits: 0; maximumFractionDigits: 0)}}). S2 calls this "{{like}}". Each +6 dB doubles the height; +20 dB is ten times.`},clapMachine:{label:`A loudness envelope over 1.2 seconds with the rise and release thresholds, a cursor, and the detector's three states with the events it has produced so far.`,which:`Which sound`,back:`One frame back`,on:`One frame on`,rise:`rise`,riseLabel:`Rise threshold in dB`,release:`release`,releaseLabel:`Release threshold in dB`,sounds:{clap:`a clap`,double:`two claps`,soft:`a soft clap`,word:`a word (“hello”)`,whistle:`a whistle`},result:{clap:`CLAP ({{ms}} ms, {{db, number(minimumFractionDigits: 0; maximumFractionDigits: 0)}} dB)`,long:`LONG {{ms}} ms: not a clap`},caption:{done:`{{sound}}: {{results}}. A clap is over within 160 ms; a voice or a whistle stays loud longer.`,loud:`Loud since {{ms}} ms: an onset already fired (games use it right away). Is it short enough to be a clap? Wait for it to fall below release.`,soft:`{{sound}}: nothing yet — this clap never reaches the rise line. Lower rise (or let S3's learn do it from your own claps).`,nothing:`{{sound}}: nothing yet.`},states:{armed:`armed`,loud:`loud`,quiet:`quiet`},arrows:{onset:`≥ rise: ONSET`,end:`< release: CLAP or LONG`,rearm:`quiet for 40 ms: re-armed`}},recorder:{label:`Nine seconds of sound: a door bang and a sentence, the trigger and hold lines, and the recorder's state as the cursor moves: armed, recording, pre-roll and the clip that is kept or dropped.`,back:`Back 0.1 seconds`,on:`On 0.1 seconds`,trigger:`trigger`,triggerLabel:`Trigger level in dB above the quiet`,hold:`hold`,holdLabel:`Hold time in milliseconds`,start:`start`,quiet:`quiet`,ring:`the ring: the last 4 s are always there (so the pre-roll is free)`,holding:`quiet {{quiet, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} of {{hold, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} s: hold`,loud:`loud: the hold restarts`,saved:`clip {{n}} saved: {{s, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} s`,dropped:`bang at {{s, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} s dropped (too short)`,caption:{rec:`Recording. The clip started half a second before the trigger (amber): the ring buffer still had it. A pause shorter than the hold keeps the sentence in one clip.`,saved:`Saved: the clip ends half a second after the last loud moment, not after the whole hold. The door bang was loud too, but under 0.4 s: dropped.`,armed:`Armed: listening, keeping nothing but the ring. Louder than the start line begins a clip.`}},getOut:{paths:{phone:{1:{title:`Open the page`,text:`Your phone opens <code>http://bench.local/</code>. The page itself is open, but holds no audio. Paste the admin token (serial <code>token</code>); it stays in this tab's sessionStorage only.`},2:{title:`List the clips`,text:`<code>GET /api/clips</code> with <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code>. Without the token: 401. Every audio route checks it.`},3:{title:`Stream one clip`,text:`<code>GET /api/clip?n=3</code>: the board sends the 44-byte header, then the samples straight from PSRAM in chunks, made louder (normalised, up to ×64) on the way. Never one big string in RAM.`},4:{title:`Draw and play`,text:`The page decodes the WAV (WebAudio), draws its shape on a canvas and plays it with a moving playhead. Live level and state arrive over the WebSocket <code>/ws</code>.`}},board:{1:{title:`Pick a clip`,text:`On the <b>clips</b> view a long press picks the next clip; or type <code>play 3</code> (any clip number), <code>play 3 0.7</code> for slower and deeper.`},2:{title:`Louder, but not too loud`,text:`The clip is made louder like the download, but at most ×16: a whisper on the KY-037 would otherwise come back as mostly hiss, at full volume.`},3:{title:`Out of I2S1`,text:`The samples go to the MAX98357A at <code>speed</code> × their rate: 0.5 = half speed, an octave down. While it plays, the detectors are deaf (the echo guard); <code>rec</code> stops playback first, and <code>play</code> is refused while recording.`},4:{title:`From the phone too`,text:`The page has <b>Play on board</b> and <b>Stop on board</b> per clip and a speed choice: <code>POST /api/play?n=3&speed=0.7</code> with the token; <code>/api/status</code> shows <code>playing</code> and <code>playProgress</code>.`}},usb:{1:{title:`Close the monitor`,text:`Only one program can hold the serial port. Close <code>pio device monitor</code> first.`},2:{title:`Ask for clip n`,text:`<code>uv run python tools/sound_pull.py /dev/cu.usbserial-A5069RR4 3</code> opens the port without resetting the board and types <code>send 3</code>.`},3:{title:`Base64 lines`,text:`The board answers <code>WAV-BEGIN 3 102444 clip-3.wav</code>, then the whole WAV as base64 text, 76 characters a line (serial is a text pipe), then <code>WAV-END &lt;crc32&gt;</code>. Nothing else is printed in between.`},4:{title:`Check and save`,text:`The tool decodes, compares the byte count and the CRC-32, and writes <code>clip-3.wav</code>. 100 kB takes about 12 s at 115 200 baud.`}}},label:`The board, the phone and the Mac, with the message of the current step travelling between them.`,path:`Path`,choices:{phone:`📱 phone (Wi-Fi)`,usb:`🔌 USB (serial)`,board:`🔈 the board's speaker`},previous:`Previous step`,next:`Next step`,position:`{{at}} / {{total}}`,caption:`<b>{{n}}. {{title}}</b> {{text}}`,msg:{chunks:`WAV, chunk by chunk`,cone:`I2S1 → class D → cone`},boxes:{clips:`clips in PSRAM`,you:`you`,serialOrButton:`serial or button`,speaker:`+ speaker`,phone:`phone`,browser:`browser`},headline:{phone:`Every audio route needs the token · the board shows REC whoever starts it`,board:`The board plays it itself: no phone, no cable`,usb:`No Wi-Fi needed · the board's USB serial port, 115 200 baud`},wavLabel:`The 44-byte WAV header`,wav:{riff:`the file type`,size:`102 436 = everything after this`,wave:`a wave file`,fmt:`the format chunk`,fmtLen:`its length`,pcm:`PCM: plain samples`,channels:`one channel (mono)`,rate:`samples a second`,byteRate:`bytes a second`,align:`bytes per sample`,bits:`bits per sample`,data:`the samples follow`,dataSize:`102 400 bytes = 3.2 s`}},whistle:{label:`Six steps from samples to a note name: the samples, the window, the FFT bins, the peak bin, the parabola between bins and the note with its cents.`,previous:`Previous step`,next:`Next step`,position:`{{at}} / {{total}}`,whistle:`whistle`,whistleLabel:`Whistle frequency in Hz`,title:`{{n}} · {{title}}`,hump:`the hump: 0 at both ends`,allBins:`all 256 bins, 0-8 kHz · below: the 25 around the peak`,peakBin:`bin {{k}} = {{hz, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} Hz · clarity {{clarity, number(minimumFractionDigits: 0; maximumFractionDigits: 0)}} dB`,steps:{samples:{title:`512 samples`,text:`The newest 512 samples from the ring: 32 ms of a whistle near 1 760 Hz, about 56 waves.`},window:{title:`× window`,text:`Multiplied by the Hann hump: zero at both ends, so the FFT sees no jump where the block repeats.`},fft:{title:`FFT`,text:`256 bins, 31.25 Hz apart. Most are near the floor; a few around 1 760 Hz stand tall.`},peak:{title:`peak bin`,text:`The tallest bin between 80 Hz and 4 kHz. Its clarity (how far above the average it stands) is well over 12 dB: a tone, not noise. Speech fails this test.`},parabola:{title:`parabola`,text:`The true pitch sits between bins. A parabola through the peak and its two neighbours puts it between them, much finer than 31 Hz.`},note:{title:`Hz → note`,text:`midi = 69 + 12 × log2(Hz / 440). The nearest whole number is the note; what's left × 100 is the cents. ±5 cents shows green on the tuner.`}}},knock:{label:`The secret rhythm and your attempt drawn as knocks on two lines, both stretched to the same length, with the gaps compared.`,intro:`Knock a rhythm of 2-12 knocks (button or Space while the picture has focus). Wait 1.5 s: it becomes the secret. Then knock it again, faster or slower.`,recording:`recording: the secret`,trying:`trying to open`,knock:`👊 knock (or Space)`,startOver:`↺ start over`,tolerance:`tolerance`,toleranceLabel:`Tolerance in percent`,saved_one:`Secret saved: {{count}} knock over {{s, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} s. Now knock it again, a bit faster or slower.`,saved_other:`Secret saved: {{count}} knocks over {{s, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} s. Now knock it again, a bit faster or slower.`,needTwo:`A secret needs 2 knocks or more.`,open:`OPEN, score {{score}}: each gap within {{pct}}% of the secret's share, whatever the speed.`,locked:`Locked: {{why}}.`,lockedWorst:`Locked: {{why}} (worst gap off by {{worst}}%).`,why:{noSecret:`no secret yet`,tooFew:`too few knocks`,tooMany:`too many knocks`,off:`the rhythm is off`},long:`{{s, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} s long`,secret:`secret`,noSecret:`no secret yet: knock one`,newSecret:`new secret…`,knocking:`knocking…`,pause:`1.5 s of quiet ends the pattern`,yours:`your knock`},fft:{label:`A sound made of up to three sine waves plus noise: its samples on top, and below the FFT's bins with their frequencies.`,tone:`tone {{n}}`,toneLabel:`Tone {{n}} frequency in Hz (0 = off)`,off:`off`,noise:`noise`,noiseLabel:`Noise amount`,size:`FFT size`,hann:`Hann window`,caption:{windowed:`With the window, each tone is a narrow peak and the floor stays low. Try two tones 40 Hz apart at N = 256, then at 1024: finer bins tell them apart, but each answer needs 64 ms of sound instead of 16.`,raw:`Without the window the samples' ends don't meet, and that jump smears every tone across all the bins (leakage). Tick Hann window again.`},samples:`{{n}} samples = {{ms}} ms of sound`,samplesWindowed:`{{n}} samples = {{ms}} ms of sound × the Hann window (dashed)`,bins:`bins {{apart, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} Hz apart · {{bins}} bins up to 8 kHz (Nyquist: half of 16 000) · strongest {{hz, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} Hz = {{note}} {{cents}} cents`},speaker:{label:`Samples going to the MAX98357A over I2S, its fast on-off pulses whose width follows each sample, the speaker cone following their average; a volume curve; and a timeline of the echo guard muting the detectors while the speaker plays.`,volume:`volume`,volumeLabel:`Volume 0 to 100`,output:`{{vol}} → ×{{gain, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} ({{db, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} dB)`,outputSilent:`{{vol}} → ×{{gain, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} (silent)`,hear:`🔈 hear 440 Hz at this volume`,caption:`Volume {{vol}}: every sample × {{gain, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} before it leaves. The amplifier's pulses are too fast for the cone, so it follows their average: the wave.`,samples:`samples (16-bit)`,classD:`MAX98357A: on/off ~300 000 times a second`,cone:`the cone`,squared:`vol → loudness (squared: 50 = a quarter)`,whySquared:`Volume is squared because ears hear ratios: 100 → 50 is −12 dB, a quarter of the power.`,whyGuard:`The mic and the speaker share the room: while the speaker plays (and 200 ms after), the detectors get no frames. A beep is never a clap. S8's live effects read the ring instead.`,guard:{title:`the echo guard: the mic hears the board's own beep`,speaker:`speaker`,mic:`mic`,lab:`to the lab`,muted:`muted: beep + 200 ms`,clap:`✓ clap`}},synth:{label:`The wave's shape, its harmonics up to 8 kHz with the ones that fold back at 16 kHz in red, and the ADSR envelope with four handles to drag.`,wave:`Wave`,waves:{sine:`sine`,square:`square`,triangle:`triangle`,saw:`saw`},keys:`Keys: play a note`,periods:`four periods`,harmonics:`harmonics, 0-8 kHz (the board's 16 kHz limit)`,adsr:`ADSR: attack, decay, sustain, release`,click:`a note that starts or stops at full height makes the cone jump: a click. The envelope fades it in and out. Drag the handles →`,what:{sine:`sine: one frequency, pure`,square:`square: odd harmonics 1, 3, 5…: hollow, like a clarinet or an old game`,triangle:`triangle: odd harmonics, falling fast: soft, flute-like`,saw:`saw: every harmonic: bright, buzzy`},caption:`{{what}}.`,captionAliased_one:`{{what}}. On the board {{count}} harmonic is above 8 kHz and folds back as a wrong note (red): the grit of a simple 16 kHz synth.`,captionAliased_other:`{{what}}. On the board {{count}} harmonics are above 8 kHz and fold back as wrong notes (red): the grit of a simple 16 kHz synth.`},parrot:{label:`The recording on top and what the parrot plays below, with a playhead.`,voice:`Voice`,fx:{normal:`normal`,chipmunk:`chipmunk`,giant:`giant`,robot:`robot`,echo:`echo`,reverse:`reverse`},play:`▶ the parrot says it`,record:`🎙 record 3 s with my microphone`,useExample:`use the example voice`,intro:`An example voice is loaded. Your own voice is optional: the microphone starts only when you press record, stops after 3 s, and the sound never leaves this page.`,noAudio:`This browser has no Web Audio.`,recording:`Recording from your microphone for 3 s… (the red dot shows it)`,recorded:`Recorded {{s, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} s, kept in this page only (made louder, like the board does). Pick a voice and press ▶.`,example:`The example voice: two syllables made of harmonics shaped like vowels.`,failed:{unsupported:`No microphone access in this browser: the example voice works the same.`,denied:`The microphone wasn't allowed (by you, the browser or this page's host). The example voice works the same.`,unreadable:`Couldn't read that recording: the example voice is still here.`},heard:`what it heard`,says:`what the parrot says ({{fx}})`,saysSpeed:`what the parrot says ({{fx}}, ×{{speed}} speed)`},kit:{hands:{clap:`👏 clap`,twoClaps:`👏👏 two claps`,talk:`🗣 talk 3 s`,whistle:`〰 whistle 3 s`,silence:`🤫 silence`},previewCaption:`A pretend microphone and speaker, drawn like the board does. The buttons stand in for your hands, your voice, the push button and the serial monitor; a click on the screen is a clap. Browser sound stays off until you switch it on.`,micToKy:`mic: INMP441 → KY-037`,micToInmp:`mic: KY-037 → INMP441`,browserOn:`🔈 hear it in the browser: on`,browserOff:`🔈 hear it in the browser: off`,tapHint:`Click or press Space to clap`,previewSoon:`Preview coming with this lab.`,screensSoon:`Coming with this lab.`},s1:{captions:{scope:`scope: the last 20 ms of sound, lined up on a rising zero crossing so a steady tone stands still. The scale follows the wave and is written in millivolts at the AO pin.`,mics:`mics: both microphones, one above the other (INMP441 on top): each one's quiet in dBFS and how far above it the sound is now, and how many dB more clearly the INMP441 hears it. Only one wired? It shows how to wire the other (then press RST).`,bias:`bias (KY-037): a 0-3.3 V ruler. Shaded = the ADC's range at this attenuation, the line = the bias, the band = how far the wave swung in the last 0.1 s. The hint says whether a smaller range would fit. With the INMP441 it becomes a ladder of its 24 bits: how many the sound uses (6 dB each) and the headroom left.`,comparator:`comparator: the DO lamp (like LED2 on the module), rising edges per second, and 6 s of loudness (top) against DO (bottom). Tune the pot until DO lights only with the loud moments. The INMP441 has no DO: the board compares in software (thresh, 3 dB of hysteresis).`,numbers:`numbers: the source, the sampler's real rate, lost samples, the level in dBFS (and dB SPL with the INMP441), the quiet, dB above it, free PSRAM.`},actions:{attenNext:`atten next`,freeze:`freeze`,potMoreSensitive:`pot: more sensitive`,potLessSensitive:`pot: less sensitive`}},s2:{captions:{meter:`meter: with the INMP441, dB SPL big (unweighted, ±2 dB) on a 30-110 scale: 30 whisper, 60 talk, 85 hearing risk, 100 loud. With the KY-037, dB above your room's quiet, what that roughly sounds like, and a bar with the peak hold (white tick) and the alarm line (dotted red).`,minute:`minute: the loudest moment of every quarter second, for the last 60 s, coloured by loudness; the dotted line is the alarm limit.`,diary:`diary: ten minutes in 60 slots of 10 s: a thin line from the quietest to the loudest moment, a block at the average, a red cap where the alarm fired. Example history plus what you do now.`,alarm:`alarm: armed or ringing, the fuse filling while it is too loud (1 s by default), how many alarms and the loudest. Any button press silences it.`},actions:{loudMusic3S:`📢 loud music 3 s`,speedFastSlow:`speed fast/slow`,buttonSilences:`button (silences)`}},s3:{captions:{timeline:`timeline: the last 4 s of loudness with the rise (dotted white) and release (dotted grey) lines. A clap gets a teal flag; a long sound (a word, a whistle) an amber bar marked long. Top left: the pattern as it forms, then what it did.`,lamp:`lamp: the LED on GPIO 4 as a bulb and the RGB LED as an orb in its colour. Clap 4 times for the party.`,patterns:`patterns: 1 to 4 claps as pairs of hands and the action each one runs; the last match lights up for 3 s.`,tune:`tune: double-click, then clap 5 times as you normally would. Each bar is one clap (dB above the quiet, how long it rang); sens becomes 60 % of the softest.`},actions:{threeClaps:`👏👏👏 three claps`,n4Party:`👏×4 party`,learn2xOnTune:`learn (2x on tune)`}},s4:{captions:{recorder:`recorder: OFF, ARMED or REC with the seconds, big. The last 3 s of loudness with the start line: amber = the pre-roll a clip took from the ring, red = what went into the clip.`,clips:`clips: up to four rows with each clip's shape, length and kind (voice or hand); on the right the picked clip's loudest moment, how much louder it is sent, and when it started. Double-click plays the picked clip on the board's speaker; a long press here picks the next one.`,web:`web: where the page is (http://bench.local), the IP, the signal, how many pages are open, and how to get the token. Without saved Wi-Fi it points to W1 or sound_pull.py.`},actions:{armOnOff:`arm on/off`,recStopLongPress:`rec / stop (long press)`,playPicked2xOn:`▶ play picked (2x on clips)`,talk2S:`🗣 talk 2 s`}},s5:{captions:{bars:`bars: 24 bands from 100 Hz to 8 kHz on a log scale (like a piano), in dB above each band's own quiet, with falling peak caps. Top: the loudest frequency and its note; right: N, Hz per bin, the FFT's time.`,waterfall:`waterfall: about 6 s of spectrum scrolling left, low notes at the bottom; night → pink → white = 0 → 40 dB above the quiet. A whistle that slides draws a line.`,tuner:`tuner: whistle or hum: the note big (teal within ±5 cents), the frequency, a needle from flat to sharp and a 5 s trail of your pitch around the note.`,steer:`steer: whistle higher to lift the glowing dot, lower to drop it (600-2500 Hz, log scale). Hold it in the ring for a second to score. The RGB LED takes the dot's colour while you whistle.`},actions:{slideWhistle4S:`〰 slide whistle 4 s`,music3S:`🎵 music 3 s`,hold:`hold`,ref2xOnTuner:`ref (2x on tuner)`,sweep:`sweep`}},s6:{captions:{knock:`knock: the padlock, the secret's rhythm on top, your last knock under it with each gap coloured (teal close, amber nearly, coral off). Double-click, knock a rhythm, wait 1.5 s: that's the secret. Open lifts the shackle, lights the LED on GPIO 4 and the RGB LED green for 5 s.`,bird:`bird: Clap Bird. Every clap is a flap; fly through the gaps. In this preview, press Space or click the screen to clap.`,party:`party: play music near the mic (the 🎵 button here). Every beat flashes a new colour, sends a ring out and lights the RGB LED; the tempo is the median gap of the last 8 beats.`},actions:{music6S:`🎵 music 6 s`,knockSet:`knock set`,knockTheSecret:`knock the secret (✊ ✊ ✊ … ✊ ✊)`,knockPlay:`knock play`}},s7:{captions:{wave:`wave: one note's waveform (four periods) and its spectrum 0-8 kHz. Same note, different wave = different timbre; the line under it says why.`,keys:`keys: two octaves, C4 to B5. Notes light while they sound. Double-click = the next note of the scale, long press = the next chord.`,envelope:`envelope: the ADSR curve (attack, decay, sustain, release); a dot rides it while a single note plays.`,tunes:`tunes: the list on the left, a piano roll on the right scrolling past the amber playhead. Double-click plays the picked tune, long press picks the next.`,fx:`fx: game sounds built from recipes (sweeps, noise, filters); the last one's whole waveform with a playhead.`},actions:{waveNext:`wave next`,tunePicked:`▶ tune picked`,fxPicked:`▶ fx picked`,longPressNext:`long press (next)`}},s8:{captions:{parrot:`parrot: say something and it says it back in the chosen voice. Listening (arcs come in), recording (wide eye, REC), talking (the beak moves with the speaker). Below: your level against the start line.`,echo:`echo: live, mic → delay line → speaker. The bars show what one clap turns into: each repeat feedback × quieter, delay apart. Bottom: in and out levels and the latency.`,robot:`robot: live, your voice times a slow sine (the carrier) plus 8-bit crunch; the mouth lights with what comes out.`,scope:`scope: the chain mic → effect → speaker, with what goes in (teal) and what comes out (pink).`},actions:{saySomething2S:`🗣 say something (2 s)`,fxNext:`fx next`,again:`again`,echoOnOff:`echo on/off`,robotOnOff:`robot on/off`}}};export{e as default};
